 we measure for bias  we'll see here that um for the attribute  protected attribute of sex  um you know the prediction actually is  66 percent  but four out of five of these metrics  show bias for sex so you can see in red  here  um these metrics should be measuring  much closer to zero and this should be  close to one  and for race we also see bias in four  out of five of these metrics  um for statistical parity difference  equal opportunity difference average  odds difference  and distributed disparate impact  so what i'm going to do here is  use the re-weighing algorithm and this  is pre-processing so this  weights the examples in each group  or label differently to ensure fairness  before classification  but as you can see down here there's uh  two different algorithms you could use  there  you could use an in processing algorithm  or  post-processing but i'm going to you  know run this  model again after re-weighing that data  set  and what you'll see here is for the  protected attribute of sex  the prediction accuracy was unchanged  you know still at 66 percent  uh but what we can see here in the  before and after  is that the bias measurement has  significantly reduced  so much closer to zero here and closer  to one for disparate impact  and this is the same for race as well  so we'll see um that prediction accuracy  hasn't changed much  but we have uh reduced bias  significantly across the board  so um with that i'm going to just go  into a quick  medical expenditure tutorial and so what  we're going to do is look at  how bias occurs in healthcare data and  how we can mitigate it  with ai fairness 360. so just to give a  an overview of this use case health  insurance companies uh  like to treat patients differently when  providing managed care  for things like say diabetes or heart  disease  so they'll use algorithms to look at  patients and their data to prioritize  cases for care management  however in the u.s whites utilize  managed care more often than  african-americans  on average and this propagates into the  data set  um and into your model as bias so  um you know first and foremost so why do  african americans spend less  on healthcare than whites so there's a  lot of research and data around this  um one of the reasons is that african  americans  may have jobs that don't allow them to  take off  for medical appointments and this is  even if they have health insurance  the second is that doctors often treat  african-americans with implicit bias and  may not believe their patient is as sick  as they're  reporting to be so less health care  treatment is prescribed  and i don't know if you follow the news  and see that saw that michael bloomberg  donated about 100 million dollars to  black medical colleges to increase the  number of black doctors  so um you know the third reason is  really african americans  may not trust uh their white doctors so  they may not trust the health care  treatment that's prescribed so  because of those three reasons you know  you have doctors decisions  and patience decisions and those two  things have created  bias in your data set so basically if  you  if your model shows that a particular  race spends more money  or less uh and uses less healthcare  services your model will be biased and  predicting how sick a patient is and  whether they need managed care  so it's important to ensure that you  have fairness in your model  uh and in your data set  so if we go down here  this is basically a demonstrating  bias and healthcare classification model  we build a classifier using  logistic regression um this notebook  does random forest models  but i'm going to just um do the logistic  regression due to time  and we're going to detect bias using  five different metrics  and then we're going to do two different  approaches to bias mitigation so  pre-processing  as well as in processing and then for  the data set  we're using the medical expenditure  uh panel survey and essentially this  is uh the most complete source of data  on the cost and use of health care  and health insurance coverage in the u.s  so it's a large set  a scale of surveys of families  medical providers and employers across  the us  so um the features that we're going to  the protected attribute that we're going  to focus on is the  uh race so the privilege class will be  defined as  white and the unprivileged class will be  defined as non-white  and there are a lot of other features in  the model  including demographics you know such as  age  gender active duty status and  also physical mental health assessments  and things like diagnosis for cancer  diabetes  and physical limitations such as  cognitive hearing or vision  so the model classification task is  really going to be to predict  whether a person has or will have high  utilization  um of of health visits so that would be  greater than  or equal to 10 visits  per year to the doctor and that's about  the average in the in the u.s  per year okay  so um the steps that we're going to take  here  are we're going to look at three panels  of data  so uh we're going to look at panel 19  panel 20 and panel 21  of people who are continuously  monitoring sampled for a year from 2014  to 2016.  um so we'll use panel 19 for training  models panel 20 for deploying  and panel 21 for retraining to correct  for the bias drift  so um basically here what we're going to  do is  import the standard python ai fairness  360  packages uh import the lime explainers  as well as scikit-learn  [Music]  and then to simulate the scenario we're  going to split the data into train  validation into chess set and again  we're defining the  privileged and unprivileged class as  whites and non-whites  and just a quick summary of the data set  there are 7915 patients  in the training set 4749 and validation  3166 in the test set and there are 138  features  and again here's the uh protected  attribute of race  but there are many many features as you  can see here we won't  go through all of those  and so first what we're going to do is  measure  bias on the original data set by using  the disparate impact metric  and you'll see that right here the  disparate impact is measured at 0.48 and  this number should actually be much  closer to 1 so this indicates that there  is bias in the original data set  so now we're going to look at the  logistic reaction model on the original  data  and uh once we do that we'll see down  here that  the prediction accuracy is good when we  use one minus discrete impact or average  odds difference  so prediction accuracy is 76 percent  uh but you find that um you know a lot  of these metrics  the level of bias is too high and should  be much closer to zero  this is just biased for the training  data so um when we look back down at the  testing data we see that  you know it's the same kind of scenario  of uh high level bias  so the point we're just trying to make  is that the original data set is unfair  and hence the model we're using  will be unfair due to that data  so we're going to skip through the  random forest models  because that's essentially the same  process that we went through  before so now i'm going to  apply re-weighing on the original data  and it associates what this is going to  do is associate a weight  to each data point so that the final  data set  you obtain is more fair so once you do  this  um you'll see you know again we're  measuring disparate impact  and after applying that algorithm  disparate impact is one  and so this is really good this  indicates that the data set is much more  fair  so again you know just going back and  training the  uh retraining the data set  with that was has been reweighed uh and  we'll see that the prediction accuracy  is gone from 0.77 to 0.75  and the level of bias is improved by an  order of magnitude  and that's for the train and test set as  well  so again we're going to skip through the  random forest part  and what we're going to do is look at an  in-processing technique  called the prejudice remover this  algorithm  changes the model by directly applying  constraints so again the in processing  algorithms directly change the model  itself versus preprocessing what we saw  before  changes the data  so um you know when we run through this  model  using the prejudice remover we see that  essentially prediction accuracy has gone  down  to from 0.77 to 68 percent  um however the bias has reduced  um significantly so you know you really  have to be careful with your approach  uh the type of approach that you use  because this did  impact the uh prediction accuracy  significantly  and right here is just kind of a summary  of results right we used um  two different types of approaches on the  original data we used re-weighing  as well as the prejudice remover so you  can see when you go back to this  notebook later  how it impacted the bias measurement and  prediction accuracy  so you know if we want to go back and  check for  um you know when we're satisfied with  our bias mitigation we can go back  and check after a year by deploying this  model on panel 20 data so  2014 data and model used in 20 000 uh  2015  um and that when we do that  we can see that um the  bias prediction accuracy went from 77 to  73  so that's pretty good um and the bias  measurements here show that  bias is still pretty good so it hasn't  drifted much over one year  and you know if i i'm going to go back  to the lime explainers  if we now look at we're going to test  the model that was trained  using panel 19 data on panel 21 data so  this is two years later  if we just take this model and redeploy  it  we'll see here that our prediction  accuracy is good  um but again the bias  increased over two years so not in the  first year uh  but it it increased during the second  year  so again we need to refresh this model  with data from 2015  and again redeploy that back out into  2016 models so we're able to correct for  that bias drift  so that's just an important thing to to  keep in mind um  that you can't just do this on the front  end you need to continuously check for  bias  um over time  and if i go back up into the lime  explainers  and i'm getting just to the end of time  here  um you know it's it's important this is  uh two different instances  um but we're going to visualize the  explanation using the lyme model  and just for two particular patients um  essentially we can see that these  features indicate what's important  and their weights that contribute to  whether a patient  uh requires more managed care or not um  so essentially the right hand side shows  the actual values of the feature in the  partic  uh particular instance um  and the associated weights so these are  things like chronic bronchitis  smoking pregnancy emphysema etc  so it's important to look at um  you know what is contributing and you  know oftentimes nurses may go back and  do this what is contributing to  a model predicting whether a patient is  high risk  um and requires more managed care  so um just a quick summary of everything  we've done here i know this is uh  very quick but essentially  the original model from 2014 was very  unfair when we deployed  it in 2016 so we retrain that model  using the latest data and this improved  the fairness and accuracy  so again really important to  continuously check for your model  and drift over time um but also  to try different approaches you know  it's best to mitigate bias on your data  set but you may not always be able to do  that  but it is important to to use as many  metrics as possible to  to ensure you're checking for bias in as  many different ways as you can  so with that um  i think the last thing i might leave you  with in chat  um is a few resources so i'll put this  in chat  and this is where you would get the  demos tutorials and notebooks  uh you can join the af 360 slack we have  a ton of researchers and developers who  answer all sort